# What each indirect call site reaches, per track, for tools/stack_depth.py.
#
# HAND-WRITTEN, and the opposite of tools/stream_seam.list in one
# important way: that file is generated from the source and a test holds
# it equal to a fresh extraction. This one cannot be. Every line here is
# a CLAIM about what a function pointer can point at, made by a person,
# and no extraction can confirm it - which is exactly why it is tracked
# rather than retyped into a shell each time. A claim in shell history
# is a claim nobody can review.
#
# Format, whitespace separated, `#` to end of line is a comment:
#
#     <track>  indirect  <location substring>  <table|noreturn|none>
#     <track>  leaf      <symbol>              <bytes>
#
# `indirect` specs, in descending order of how much they are worth:
#
#   <symbol>   a const dispatch table. EXACT - the tool reads the array
#              out of the linked ELF, masks the Thumb bit and maps the
#              addresses back through the symbol table, so the target
#              set is re-derived from every build and cannot drift from
#              the source the way an annotation can.
#   noreturn   the site does not come back, so it contributes no chain.
#   none       no target is ever registered. The weakest of the three
#              and the only one resting on nothing but the writer: it
#              needs a reason on the line, and the reason needs to name
#              what would falsify it.
#
# A site with no line here is REFUSED, not assumed. That is the point of
# the file: adding a function pointer to this firmware breaks the build
# report until somebody says what it reaches.

# --- Track B -----------------------------------------------------------
# The console's 50-letter dispatch. `console_bindings` is `const`, so the
# table is in .rodata and resolves exactly: 50 entries, 50 targets, 0
# unknown.
b  indirect  console.c:260        console_bindings
# `f` proves the fault handler by branching to an address that is not
# code. It faults rather than returning, so there is no chain past it.
b  indirect  console_cmds.c:44    noreturn

# --- Track C -----------------------------------------------------------
# Same two, from the same shared source.
c  indirect  console.c:260        console_bindings
c  indirect  console_cmds.c:44    noreturn
# FreeRTOS's software-timer dispatch, three sites in timers.c. This build
# sets configUSE_TIMERS 1 - so the timer task exists and the callback
# call sites are compiled - but nothing anywhere calls xTimerCreate, so
# no callback is ever registered and the list the sites walk is empty.
#
# WHAT WOULD FALSIFY THIS: the first xTimerCreate() in apps/rtos_bringup.
# At that point this line is wrong and the honest replacement is a table
# spec naming wherever the callbacks come from, or nothing at all - and
# the tool refusing is the correct outcome until one exists.
c  indirect  timers.c             none

# --- Track A -----------------------------------------------------------
# Nothing yet, and the reason written here first was WRONG. It said the
# vendored core is compiled `-w` so -fcallgraph-info does not reach it.
# `-w` inhibits warnings and has nothing to do with this flag; measured,
# the flag reaches the core fine - 79 .ci files, 58 of them from
# track_a_core. That claim was asserted rather than tested, which is the
# thing CLAUDE.md says to do to "the core will not let us" before
# believing it.
#
# Two real reasons, and only the second is Track A's own.
#
# C++ NAME MANGLING. Track A is C++, so GCC writes the DEMANGLED
# signature into the .ci label - `void watchdogSetup()`,
# `RingBuffer::RingBuffer()` - while nm and `objdump --disassemble=`
# want the mangled symbol. Every leaf lookup misses and the tool
# refuses. 12 of the 33 unresolved leaves are only this, and are
# defined in the image. It is the same defect issue #45 hit doing
# dead-function analysis on this track, recurring in a new tool: a
# plain-name match against C++ is wrong in both directions.
#
# NEWLIB, which is the substantive one. Track A links the C library and
# calls into it - malloc, free, atof, memcmp, memmove, isspace - and
# none of those is compiled here, so 21 leaves have no frame anywhere in
# the tree. Track B avoids the C library on the working path and has
# six. That is a real difference between the tracks rather than a
# tooling gap, and closing it means declaring those frames with `leaf`
# lines, each carrying how it was measured.
#
# So Track A is reachable, at the cost of demangling plus 21 measured
# declarations. It is debt with a date on it, not a property of the
# track.
